# ip-bar configuration
#
# Copy this file to ".env" in the same folder and edit the values you need.
# EVERY setting is optional — with no .env at all the widget runs fine using
# the defaults shown below. Nothing in THIS file is a secret: these are file
# paths and hostnames. The widget never asks you for an API key. (One setting,
# AI_ACCOUNTS_FILE, points at a file that does hold credentials — the path is
# not sensitive, the file it names is.)
#
#   copy .env.example .env
#
# Restart the widget after editing (see README "Restarting").


# ── AI usage panel ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The widget reads the OAuth token that the Claude Code and Codex CLIs already
# wrote when you logged in. It never sees your password and never stores a
# token itself. If you do not use these CLIs, leave this section alone — the
# AI rows will simply say "setup" and everything else keeps working.
#
# By default it looks in your Windows user profile:
#     %USERPROFILE%\.claude\.credentials.json
#     %USERPROFILE%\.codex\auth.json
#
# If you run the CLIs inside WSL, the tokens live in your Linux home instead.
# Point the widget at them here. Separate multiple paths with ";".
#
# Order does not decide the winner: the widget reads every path it can and
# uses the one whose token expires LATEST, so listing a stale leftover copy
# alongside a live one is harmless. Copies older than the refresh-token
# lifetime (16 days) are ignored entirely — if every copy is that old the
# panel says "credentials stale" rather than claiming your login expired.
#
# Replace "Ubuntu" with your distro name and "youruser" with your Linux user:

#CLAUDE_CRED_PATHS=\\wsl.localhost\Ubuntu\home\youruser\.claude\.credentials.json
#CODEX_CRED_PATHS=\\wsl.localhost\Ubuntu\home\youruser\.codex\auth.json

# Both CLIs, listing WSL first and falling back to Windows:
#CLAUDE_CRED_PATHS=\\wsl.localhost\Ubuntu\home\youruser\.claude\.credentials.json;C:\Users\You\.claude\.credentials.json


# ── AI account vault ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The CLIs above hold one login each. The widget keeps its own copy of every
# account you register, so several accounts of the same vendor can be tracked
# at once. Those copies live in one JSON file, the vault. On first run the
# widget imports whatever the CLIs already have; after that you add accounts
# from the widget's AI card.
#
# Default location:
#     %APPDATA%\net-watch-ui\accounts.json
#
# THE VAULT CONTAINS LIVE CREDENTIALS. It is created with owner-only
# permissions. Do not put it in a synced or shared folder, and do not paste it
# anywhere. Deleting it only loses the account list; the CLIs' own logins are
# untouched and can be imported again.
#
# Set this to move it — onto an encrypted volume, for instance. "~" and
# %VARIABLES% are expanded. The CLAUDE_CRED_PATHS / CODEX_CRED_PATHS settings
# above are unaffected: they stay the place the widget IMPORTS from, while this
# is where it keeps what it imported.

#AI_ACCOUNTS_FILE=%APPDATA%\net-watch-ui\accounts.json


# ── Re-login command ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# When a login really has expired the AI panel marks that account "login
# expired — click to sign in". Clicking it, or adding a new account with the
# + button, opens a console running the command below so you can finish the
# CLI's own browser sign-in. The widget never handles your password; all the
# click saves is opening a terminal and typing the command yourself.
#
# "offline" is NOT this state and does not offer a sign-in: it means the
# request never reached the provider, and your credential is untouched.
#
# Defaults are the bare CLI names, which assumes the CLIs are on your Windows
# PATH:
#     AI_LOGIN_CMD_CLAUDE=claude
#     AI_LOGIN_CMD_CODEX=codex login
#
# Override them if they are not — for example when the CLIs live inside WSL.
# Replace "Ubuntu" with your distro name:

#AI_LOGIN_CMD_CLAUDE=wsl.exe -d Ubuntu -- bash -lic claude
#AI_LOGIN_CMD_CODEX=wsl.exe -d Ubuntu -- bash -lic "codex login"


# ── Adding a SECOND account (isolated login) ──────────────────────────────────
# Every one of these CLIs stores exactly ONE login. Running the vendor's login
# command again to add a second account does not prompt you again: the CLI
# finds the session already sitting in your home directory and hands back the
# SAME account. So the + button does not just run the command — it runs it
# inside a DISPOSABLE SANDBOX, a temporary directory handed to the CLI as its
# home. The CLI finds no existing session there, performs a genuine fresh
# sign-in, writes the new credential inside the sandbox, and the widget copies
# that into the vault and deletes the sandbox. Your existing working login is
# never read and never overwritten.
#
# Everything below is per provider: append _CLAUDE or _CODEX to the name. The
# built-in values are correct for the current CLIs, so the whole section can be
# ignored. It exists because vendors rename these things, and a rename should
# be fixable by editing one line here rather than by waiting for a new build.
#
# Nothing here is a secret. These are VARIABLE NAMES and relative paths — never
# put a token, an API key or a proxy URL in this file.


# Which variables are pointed at the sandbox so the CLI treats it as home.
# Semicolon separated. HOME is the baseline, because relocating HOME relocates
# the home directory on every Unix-like system and that is where both CLIs
# actually run. The widget always also overrides USERPROFILE, HOMEDRIVE,
# HOMEPATH and the four XDG_*_HOME variables; this setting only adds to that
# universal set. Change it if a CLI is documented to read some other variable.
# Defaults: HOME (both providers).

#AI_LOGIN_HOME_VARS_CLAUDE=HOME
#AI_LOGIN_HOME_VARS_CODEX=HOME

# The provider's own "put my configuration here" variable, set to the sandbox's
# config directory. Some CLIs honour this and ignore HOME, so both are set and
# the CLI may pick either. Set it to an EMPTY value to disable it entirely —
# unlike the other settings, empty here means "do not set this variable" rather
# than "use the default".
# Defaults: CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR, CODEX_HOME.

#AI_LOGIN_CONFIG_VAR_CLAUDE=CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR
#AI_LOGIN_CONFIG_VAR_CODEX=CODEX_HOME

# Where that config directory sits inside the sandbox, relative to the sandbox
# root. Change it only if a CLI moves its configuration folder.
# Defaults: .claude, .codex

#AI_LOGIN_CONFIG_REL_CLAUDE=.claude
#AI_LOGIN_CONFIG_REL_CODEX=.codex

# Where the widget expects the new credential to appear, relative to the
# sandbox root. If a CLI renames its credential file a login will look like it
# timed out even though it succeeded — correct the name here. "/" works on
# either OS, and ".." is refused, because this path is the only thing keeping
# the sandbox from reaching your real credential file.
# Defaults: .claude/.credentials.json, .codex/auth.json

#AI_LOGIN_CRED_REL_CLAUDE=.claude/.credentials.json
#AI_LOGIN_CRED_REL_CODEX=.codex/auth.json

# Variables to UNSET inside the shell, just before the CLI starts. Semicolon
# separated. This is a REMOVAL list — the only one in this file.
#
# It exists because a login command like the WSL examples above runs through
# "bash -lic", which sources your ~/.bashrc first. If your rc file exports a
# proxy base URL and an auth token for one of these vendors, the CLI starts up
# already believing it is authenticated against that proxy, never opens a
# browser, and the sign-in silently does nothing. Scrubbing those names for
# THIS ONE CHILD PROCESS makes the real OAuth flow run. Your rc files are not
# edited, your normal shells are unaffected, and nothing outside this login is
# touched.
#
# Only applies to a command that goes through wsl.exe with "bash -c"/"-lic":
# that is the only point downstream of the rc file, so it is the only place the
# removal can happen at all.
#
# Set this if your shell exports some other variable that puts a CLI into
# API-key mode. Defaults:
#   CLAUDE: ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL;ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN;ANTHROPIC_API_KEY;CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN
#   CODEX:  OPENAI_API_KEY;OPENAI_BASE_URL

#AI_LOGIN_UNSET_VARS_CLAUDE=ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL;ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN;ANTHROPIC_API_KEY;CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN
#AI_LOGIN_UNSET_VARS_CODEX=OPENAI_API_KEY;OPENAI_BASE_URL

# The subcommand that actually performs a sign-in, appended to the login
# command above when that command does not already end in it. Space separated.
#
# Running the bare CLI name opens its interactive session instead of signing
# in, so a sandbox login would sit there forever waiting for a credential that
# is never written. "claude auth login" is the real sign-in invocation, so it
# is appended for Claude. The Codex default command already ends in "login",
# so nothing is appended there and this is empty.
#
# Leave it alone unless a vendor renames its login subcommand — or set it to
# empty if you have already put the subcommand into AI_LOGIN_CMD_* yourself
# (the widget also detects that case and will not duplicate it).
# Defaults: "auth login" for CLAUDE, empty for CODEX.

#AI_LOGIN_SUBCOMMAND_CLAUDE=auth login
#AI_LOGIN_SUBCOMMAND_CODEX=


# ── Network cut button ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The ◉ button disables every physical network adapter — wired and wireless —
# and a second click enables them again. Each toggle raises one UAC prompt,
# because disabling an adapter needs administrator rights and the widget itself
# runs as a normal user. Declining the prompt leaves the network untouched.
#
# Adapters whose description contains any of these substrings are never
# touched. The defaults protect hypervisor switches (disabling one takes your
# VMs and containers offline with it) and VPN tunnel adapters (their client
# owns them and misbehaves if something else disables them). Semicolon
# separated, case-insensitive; set it to extend or replace the list.

#NET_SKIP=hyper-v;vmware;virtualbox;openvpn;tap-windows;wintun;bluetooth;loopback;wan miniport;teredo

# Where the widget remembers which adapters it cut, so a restore after a
# restart puts back exactly that set.

#NET_STATE_FILE=%USERPROFILE%\.ipbar_net_state.json


# ── Ping targets ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The two hosts shown in the LATENCY row. Defaults are Cloudflare and Google
# DNS. Point them at your gateway or a work server if that is more useful.

#PING_HOST_1=1.1.1.1
#PING_HOST_2=8.8.8.8


# ── Log file ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Where the widget appends its log. Default is ip_bar_log.txt in your home
# folder. "Open Log" in the right-click menu opens whatever this points at.

#LOG_FILE=%USERPROFILE%\ip_bar_log.txt


# ── Widget size ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Not a setting any more. Drag the grip in the widget's bottom-right corner to
# scale it, and double-click that grip to go back to 100%; the size is
# remembered between runs, and the mini bar inherits it.
#
# WIDGET_TARGET_PPI, WIDGET_SCALE_PRIMARY and WIDGET_SCALE_SECONDARY used to
# live here. They belonged to the Tk build, which measured each monitor's real
# pixel density from the panel's EDID and sized itself to occupy the same
# physical amount of screen everywhere. The React build does not do that and
# ignores all three.
